{"qid":"Q1000","names":["Gabon"],"title":"Gabon","endonym":{"native":"République gabonaise","lang":"fr"},"capsule":"Gabon gained independence from France on 17 August 1960 and for four decades was dominated by Omar Bongo Ondimba, who ruled from 1967 until his death in 2009 and presided over an oil economy that generated one of sub-Saharan Africa's higher per-capita incomes while concentrating wealth among the elite. His son Ali Bongo Ondimba succeeded him and was re-elected in a disputed 2016 poll. On 30 August 2023 the military overthrew Ali Bongo hours after the announcement of his third election victory, ending six decades of Bongo family rule; a transitional military council assumed power pending elections.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Modern national boundaries are surveyed and unambiguous; the dataset's to-date of 2025 reflects ongoing post-coup transitional status.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Gabon","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Gabon"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Gabon","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gabon"}],"britannica":"place/Gabon","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Gabon","rulers":[{"qid":"Q299629","name":"Léon M'ba","start":1960,"end":1964,"image":"Leon M'ba, 1962. D789-083 (cropped).jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Leon%20M'ba%2C%201962.%20D789-083%20(cropped).jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"Public domain","artist":"Israeli GPO photographer","attributionRequired":false},"nativeName":"Léon Mba","nativeLang":"fr"},{"qid":"Q256998","name":"Jean-Hilaire Aubame","start":1964,"end":1964,"image":"Aubame.jpg","imageUrl":"http